## Mutation checks — production code temporarily reverted one at a time, then restored

 1. re-add `balance_due` to the AP INSERT               -> k015: 5 failed | 3 passed (8)
 2. re-add `balance_due` to the AR INSERT               -> k089: 5 failed | 3 passed (8)
 3. `external_ref` -> `reference` in the AR INSERT      -> k089: 5 failed | 3 passed (8)
 4. drop `external_system` from the AR INSERT           -> k089: 1 failed | 7 passed (8)
 5. AP per-row savepoint removed (plain IIFE)           -> k015: 2 failed | 6 passed (8)
 6. AR per-row savepoint removed (plain IIFE)           -> k089: 2 failed | 6 passed (8)
 7. AP settlement leg removed                           -> k015: 2 failed | 6 passed (8)
 8. AR settlement leg removed                           -> k089: 2 failed | 6 passed (8)
 9. AP `ON CONFLICT` arbiter removed (target-less)      -> k015: 8 passed (8)  <- see note
 9b. migration 625's unique index DROPped               -> k015: 5 failed | 3 passed (8)
10. AR duplicate-reason error push removed              -> k089: 1 failed | 7 passed (8)
11. amount-consistency check removed (AP)               -> k015: 1 failed | 7 passed (8)
12. `steps_completed` back to whole-value replacement   -> k015: 1 failed | 7 passed (8)
13. `errors` back to whole-value overwrite              -> k089: 1 failed | 7 passed (8)
14. request schema amounts/dates weakened to `z.any()`  -> unit: 4 failed | 7 passed (11)

Note on 9: the index, not the arbiter clause, is what makes the duplicate guard work — a
target-less `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` fires on any unique index, so removing the target alone
changes nothing once 625 is applied. 9b drops the index instead and takes the tests red, which
is the check that matters and also proves 625 must be applied before this code ships.

All mutations restored; the run above is 16/16 green.

